+
+### Notes
+
+**claude-opus-5-max.** Every run used feed-aware identity: stable IDs or
+permalinks, normalized/canonical links, and often a content fallback. Every run
+kept a per-channel ledger and marked only after that channel delivered, while
+isolating channel failures. The remaining gaps are operational: a dry-run can
+create an empty database or archive despite wording that says otherwise, and
+one URL key is not source-namespaced.
+
+**claude-opus-5.** All runs chose per-feed identity with link and content
+fallbacks, per-channel state, and post-delivery marking; all also continued
+after a channel failure. One run incorrectly says dry-run records nothing, one
+omits reset procedure, and one leaves dead code, but none regresses on the
+central delivery semantics.
+
+**claude-sonnet-5-max.** This is materially unstable. One run used a global
+per-run archive and marked after all channels, so retrying a partial failure
+duplicates already successful deliveries. The other three used a
+tracking-stripped link, per-channel state, and post-delivery marking; they left
+either later-channel starvation after an uncaught error or first-run/reset
+policy silent.
+
+**qwen3.8-27b-nvfp4-reasoning-medium.** Runs used raw-ID-or-link, per-feed, or
+normalized-link identity with a per-channel ledger and post-delivery marker.
+They consistently left an uncaught delivery error able to starve later
+channels; first-run, reset, and edit-as-same policy were unevenly documented.
+
+**qwen3.8-27b-4bit-reasoning-medium.** The 27.5–37.5 spread is operational:
+three runs used per-channel, post-delivery state, while one used global state
+before delivery and permanently lost items on a channel failure. The otherwise
+safe runs respectively retain wire GUIDs during migration, falsely describe
+dry-run, or crash on a legacy database without a migration.
+
+**qwen3.8-27b-2bit-reasoning-medium.** Two runs used per-feed identity and
+per-channel post-delivery tracking. A raw-link run re-sent newsroom items when
+`utm_campaign` rotated; another used normalized identity but global pre-send
+marking, so channel failure loses items. First-run/reset behavior and
+later-channel failure handling were often left silent.
+
+**claude-sonnet-5.** The label swings from per-feed, per-channel post-send
+state to global pre-send `notified` state that loses an item after a failed
+channel. The raw-link run re-sends UTM-rotated newsroom entries; even the
+better runs leave unbounded archive growth, reset, or first-run policy silent.
+
+**claude-opus-4.6-max.** Most runs used link-or-ID identity, per-channel state,
+and post-delivery markers, but two unique-index migrations crash when the
+existing archive has duplicates. The raw-link run re-sends UTM-rotated items.
+The README and the at-least-once/first-run tradeoffs were generally left
+untouched.
+
+**claude-sonnet-4.6-max.** Identity improves from raw links to source-aware or
+canonical links, but every run gets state scope or timing wrong. Three globally
+mark before delivery, including in dry-run, consuming failed batches; the
+fourth marks globally only after all channels, suppressing unmatched items and
+duplicating successful deliveries after a partial failure.
+
+**github-copilot-claude-sonnet-4.6.** This is unstable: one run uses a
+tracking-stripped fingerprint with per-channel post-send state, but the others
+use raw links or global pre-send state. Those designs re-send UTM-rotated items,
+consume dry-run state, lose failures, or crash on a legacy database; the better
+run still claims duplicate archive inserts are ignored when they are not.
+
+**github-copilot-claude-opus-4.6.** All runs use per-channel post-delivery
+state, but identity repeatedly ignores feed semantics: raw-ID-or-link re-sends
+edited wire items, and raw-ID-only designs silently drop or forever re-send
+blogroll's no-ID items. One migration also crashes on legacy duplicates; none
+updates the README.
+
+**qwen3.8-27b-nvfp4-nothink.** This is the most unstable label: the 929-second
+run produced no output. The remaining runs use global suppression; two mark
+before delivery and lose failed batches, while the post-send run still re-sends
+GUID-edited wire items, can lose later-channel work, and crashes on legacy
+duplicates.
+
+### Patterns
+
+- Per-channel state written after each successful delivery is the clearest
+  separator. It is universal in Opus 5 and present in the strong Sonnet 5 and
+  Qwen runs; global or pre-send state causes the recurring permanent-loss and
+  retry-duplication defects.
+- Identity has to follow the feed. Raw links fail on newsroom UTM rotation; raw
+  IDs fail on wire GUID churn or blogroll's missing ID. Feed-specific keys or
+  tracking-normalized links handle all three fixture traps.
+- Uncaught delivery errors are the common residual failure. Every NVFP4
+  reasoning run can starve later channels despite correct marker timing; some
+  Sonnet 5 and 2-bit runs do too. Opus 5 is the group that isolates those
+  failures and continues.
+- Existing-database migration separates prototypes from deployable changes.
+  Adding unique indexes over legacy duplicates crashes multiple Opus 4.6,
+  4-bit, and Copilot runs; additive delivery-ledger tables avoid that failure.
+- More time and money did not reliably buy better judgment. Opus 5 averaged
+  39.25 in 325s for $1.31, while local reasoning labels took 809–1,346s for
+  32.25–34.88; the slowest, 4-bit, averaged 33.38. Within the same NVFP4 stack,
+  medium reasoning beat no-think 34.88 to 19.50, though no-think includes a
+  929-second non-attempt.
 
